<Doc title="The .bdoc format" subtitle="what these files are, and the rules" id="theFormat">

<Section title="Why a format at all">
<Text>The pinouts used to be C++ arrays in <Code>hub/src/pinout.cxx</Code>,
drawn by code written for them. That works exactly once. The second component
needs either a second drawing routine or a generalisation of the first, and by
the fourth the hub is carrying a small documentation system that nobody planned
and nobody can add to without rebuilding.</Text>

<Text>These are <Bold>files</Bold>. Adding a component is adding one, and the
hub does not change. Nothing is compiled in, so a wiring note can be fixed
without a build - which matters, because a wiring note is usually being fixed
while the wires are in your hands.</Text>
</Section>

<Section title="A very small subset of XML">
<Text>Deliberately small. Elements, attributes, text, and comments - that is the
whole language. No namespaces, no DTD, no processing instructions, no CDATA, no
entities beyond the four that make the four reserved characters writable.</Text>

<List>
<Item>Elements are <Bold>PascalCase</Bold>: <Code>Doc</Code>,
<Code>Section</Code>, <Code>Pinout</Code>, <Code>Pin</Code>.</Item>
<Item>Attributes are <Bold>camelCase</Bold>: <Code>title</Code>,
<Code>subtitle</Code>, <Code>class</Code>, <Code>from</Code>.</Item>
<Item>Class values are <Bold>camelCase</Bold> too: <Code>power</Code>,
<Code>ground</Code>, <Code>serial</Code>, <Code>audio</Code>.</Item>
<Item>Attribute values are always in double quotes.</Item>
<Item><Code>&lt;Name/&gt;</Code> closes itself. Anything else needs
<Code>&lt;/Name&gt;</Code>.</Item>
</List>

<Text>The case rules are not decoration. An element and an attribute are
different kinds of thing and reading one as the other is the commonest mistake
in a hand-written format; making them visibly different means the mistake is
visible too.</Text>
</Section>

<Section title="Structure">
<Text>One <Code>Doc</Code> at the root. Inside it, <Code>Section</Code> and
<Code>Pinout</Code> in whatever order the component wants to be explained
in.</Text>

<Code block="yes">&lt;Doc title="..." subtitle="..." id="camelCase"&gt;
  &lt;Section title="..."&gt;
    &lt;Text&gt;a paragraph&lt;/Text&gt;
    &lt;List&gt;&lt;Item&gt;a bullet&lt;/Item&gt;&lt;/List&gt;
    &lt;Note&gt;quieter aside&lt;/Note&gt;
    &lt;Warn&gt;the one that matters&lt;/Warn&gt;
  &lt;/Section&gt;

  &lt;Pinout title="..." package="DIP-16"&gt;
    &lt;Column side="left" from="1" to="8"&gt;
      &lt;Pin n="1" name="VCC" class="power"&gt;what it is&lt;/Pin&gt;
    &lt;/Column&gt;
  &lt;/Pinout&gt;
&lt;/Doc&gt;</Code>
</Section>

<Section title="Inline">
<Text>Inside any text you may write <Code>&lt;Bold&gt;</Code>,
<Code>&lt;Dim&gt;</Code> and <Code>&lt;Code&gt;</Code>. That is the entire
markup vocabulary, and it is short on purpose: a documentation format that grows
italics and underline and colour grows a stylesheet, and then the question stops
being what a component does and starts being how it looks.</Text>

<Text>So: <Bold>bold for the thing you must not miss</Bold>,
<Dim>dim for the aside you may skip</Dim>, and <Code>Code for a literal you
would type or read on a silkscreen</Code>.</Text>
</Section>

<Section title="Pin classes and what they colour">
<Text>A <Code>Pin</Code> carries a <Code>class</Code>, and the class picks the
colour. The palette is the same sixteen ANSI colours the rest of the app draws
in, so a pinout on this screen and a plot on the next one mean the same thing by
the same colour.</Text>

<List>
<Item><Code>power</Code> and <Code>ground</Code> - structural, and quiet. They
are on every component and carry no information about this one.</Item>
<Item><Code>serial</Code>, <Code>digital</Code>, <Code>analog</Code>,
<Code>usb</Code>, <Code>audio</Code> - what the pin is for.</Item>
<Item><Code>unused</Code> - present on the package, not used here.</Item>
</List>

<Text>A pin may also carry <Code>check="yes"</Code>, which marks it as something
to verify against the silkscreen rather than trust. That is not styling: it is a
claim about confidence, and the DFPlayer's speaker pins are exactly why it
exists.</Text>
</Section>

<Section title="Rendered, or read">
<Text>Both. These open in the Code view like any other file, and the toggle
above the editor swaps between the rendered page and the source that produced
it.</Text>

<Text>That is the point of a format over a binary: the thing on screen and the
thing in the file are the same object, and when the render looks wrong you can
see immediately whether the document is wrong or the renderer is.</Text>
</Section>

</Doc>
